WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.559
Idea. A logo is made of curved and straight parts. To find its area, cut it into pieces you know: sectors, which are slices of a circle, and triangles. Then add or subtract.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:26.410
Theta is in radians. Sector area is a half r squared theta. Arc length is r theta. A triangle with sides a and b, and angle C between them, has area a half a b sine C.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:40.196
A segment is the region between a chord and its arc. A chord joins two points on a circle. The segment is the sector minus the triangle: a half r squared, times theta minus sine theta.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:52.887
Diagrams often hide equilateral triangles. Their three angles are each pi over three. Sine of pi over three, and of two pi over three, are both root three over two.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:59.145
So you find the area or perimeter of a region with arcs and straight lines, often exactly.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:11.286
Method. Step one: split the region into sectors and triangles. Step two: find each angle in radians, using geometry such as equilateral triangles.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:22.014
Step three: work out each piece. Step four: add or subtract, counting repeated pieces the right number of times. Keep exact values if asked.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.852
The trap: counting a piece once when it appears twice.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:44.430
Worked example. June twenty twenty two. Two circles each have radius six centimetres. Each centre lies on the other circle. They meet at A and B and enclose the region R. Find its exact area P, in the form a pi minus b root c. Seven marks.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:00.187
Here is the figure. O one O two is six, and so are O one A and O two A. So the triangle is equilateral, and angle A O one O two is pi over three. By symmetry, angle A O one B is two pi over three.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:11.827
The region is two equal segments, cut off by the chord A B. Sector O one A B is a half, times thirty six, times two pi over three. That is twelve pi.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:20.479
Triangle O one A B is a half, times six, times six, times sine two pi over three. That is nine root three.

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:30.351
A tempting wrong route stops at one segment, twelve pi minus nine root three. That is only half of the region. The region has two segments.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:40.913
So P is twice the segment: twenty four pi minus eighteen root three. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:53.259
Common mistake. A common mistake is a calculator in degree mode. Here angles are in radians, so sine of two pi over three is about nought point eight six six.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:10.870
Your turn. Now you try, from June twenty twenty five. Sector O A B C has radius six centimetres and perimeter twelve plus pi. The shaded region is bounded by the arc and the line A C. Find its exact area S. Six marks.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.721
Here is Figure one, the sector with the shaded region. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:30.666
Answer. Here is the figure. The perimeter is twelve plus six theta, so theta is pi over six. The shaded region is a segment: the sector minus triangle O A C.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:44.117
The sector is a half, times thirty six, times pi over six, which is three pi. The triangle is a half, times thirty six, times a half, which is nine. So S is three pi minus nine.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:51.153
The common wrong answer is three pi. That is the whole sector. It forgets to subtract the triangle.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.493
Remember: split, find angles, work out each piece, then add or subtract.
